## Core Research Claim

Existing online interactive perception systems (Martín-Martín & Brock 
IJRR 2019, Buchanan et al. Autonomous Robots) estimate kinematics and 
Coulomb friction but explicitly assume inertial dynamics are negligible. 
This is not an oversight — it is a direct physical consequence of 
quasi-static interaction, which makes inertial parameters (mass m, center 
of mass CoM, inertia tensor I) structurally unobservable. Specifically:

    τ = I·θ̈ + μ·sign(θ̇)

Under quasi-static motion θ̈ ≈ 0, so I disappears from the signal 
entirely regardless of estimator sophistication. This is an 
information-theoretic result, not a modeling choice.

The proposed solution: an online estimator that jointly recovers kinematic 
AND inertial parameters by fusing proprioception + wrist F/T through 
deliberately, minimally, safely non-quasi-static interaction — feeding an 
adaptive impedance controller that generalizes to unseen objects without 
hand-tuning.

---

## Key Differentiators from Prior Work

vs. Martín-Martín & Brock: they estimate stiction + kinetic friction only 
under quasi-static assumption — inertia explicitly assumed negligible in 
their own words. No mass, CoM, or inertia tensor estimated.

vs. Buchanan et al.: F/T used only geometrically to disambiguate joint 
type (revolute vs prismatic) — never to estimate a physical parameter. 
Hand-tuned force ceiling per object flagged as their own limitation.

vs. Endres, Trinkle & Burgard (IROS 2013) — closest prior art, must 
engage directly:
  1. They estimate a lumped scalar moment of inertia only — not decomposed 
     m, CoM, I
  2. Offline per-door learning from prior swings of the same door — not 
     online generalization
  3. Assumes kinematics already known from a separate method (Sturm et al.)
  4. No controller coupling — model used for open-loop swing planning only

vs. Karayiannidis et al.: explicitly assumes negligible door inertial 
dynamics — a named, citable limitation.

