## The Physical Model Being Fitted

Newton-Euler for a revolute hinge:

    τ(t) = I·θ̈(t) + μ·sign(θ̇(t))

Written as a linear regression at each timestep k:

    τ_k = φ_k^T · π

where:
    φ_k = [θ̈_k, sign(θ̇_k)]^T    (regressor, built from measurements)
    π = [I, μ]^T                   (unknown parameters)

This is linear-in-parameters → unique global solution, no local minima. 
RLS (Recursive Least Squares) solves it online with updates every 2ms.

Identifiability requires the information matrix to be full rank:

    Φ = Σ φ_k φ_k^T = [[Σθ̈_k², Σθ̈_k·sign(θ̇_k)],
                         [Σθ̈_k·sign(θ̇_k), N]]

Quasi-static: Σθ̈_k² ≈ 0 → rank 1 → I unobservable (condition number ~1116)
Excited:      Σθ̈_k² > 0 → rank 2 → both I and μ identifiable (cond ~2.6)

I and μ are nearly orthogonal in the regression because θ̈ oscillates 
around zero (mean ≈ 0) while sign(θ̇) ≈ +1 throughout opening — their 
inner product ≈ 0. This means a wrong friction guess has almost no effect 
on the inertia estimate.

