## Simulation Progress (what has been built and validated)

PHASE 0 (NumPy toy model):
- Single revolute door, closed-form simulation
- Quasi-static: I_err = 83.7%, condition number = 21
- Excited: I_err = 0.9%, condition number = 2
- Proves the observability claim analytically

PHASE 0b (MuJoCo + online RLS + adaptive impedance controller):
- Real 3D physics engine, door model with realistic geometry and density
- KUKA iiwa 14 added to scene, end-effector welded to handle site via 
  equality constraint (no gripper yet)
- Oracle θ replaced with FK-derived θ from arm joint angles
- Quasi-static: I_err = 99.1%, condition number ~1116
- Excited: I_err = 0.3%, condition number ~2.6
- Tracking RMSE = 0.0043 rad, zero velocity reversals
- Convergence to <10% error at t ≈ 0.14s
- Confirms Phase 0 effect holds in real physics engine with closed-loop 
  controller

PHASE 0c (raw sensor noise sensitivity sweep):
- Added Gaussian noise to θ (σ = 0.5°–2.0°) and F/T (σ = 0.05–0.1 N·m)
- Result: F/T noise alone doesn't matter — estimator robust across all 
  tested τ noise levels
- Angle noise destroys estimator — finite differencing of noisy angle 
  amplifies noise catastrophically
- Conclusion: proprioceptive FK (clean signal from arm encoders) is the 
  correct angle source, not vision-differentiated angle

FRICTION SWEEP (Phase 0b extension):
- Swept μ_true from 0.5 to 7.0 N·m (14× range)
- Fixed wrong initial guess μ_init = 3.0 N·m for all conditions
- I_err stayed below 1.3% across all friction values
- μ_err worst case 7.3% at μ_true = 0.5 N·m (6× initialization error)
- Convergence time: 0.24s–1.39s across all conditions
- Confirms inertia and friction are nearly orthogonal in the regression

